{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3d7","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c765-6a15-7930-81af-9d81d9b3108a"},"sequence":70,"recorded_at":1780531400000135,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"eb24712a-98eb-41ba-85bf-fe73b2577481","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"eb24712a-98eb-41ba-85bf-fe73b2577481","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"eb24712a-98eb-41ba-85bf-fe73b2577481"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c765-9b3b-7380-afdf-7d2ca0158c8f"},"task_id":"01a0c765-9b3b-7380-afdf-7d2ca0158c8f","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c765-9b3b-7380-afdf-7d2ca0158c8f","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-3-1\",\n  \"command\": \"cat \\\"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev2.md\\\"; echo \\\"=====REVIEWER-CR2=====\\\"; cat \\\"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_reviewer-cr2.md\\\"; echo \\\"=====REWORK-REV3=====\\\"; cat \\\"/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev3.md\\\"\",\n  \"description\": \"Read rework and reviewer docs\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer.md. Do exactly what is written here.\\nRework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz, the SECOND of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i. Revision 1 was reviewed by RUN-260922-3770c1 (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **five P1**, two P2. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev1.md in full first — every finding names a production file and line, a reviewer test that fails through the production entry, and a pinned SPEC line.\\nWhat holds: all 8 operation entries are exercised through `Lifecycle.Execute`; B18 length checks exist and pass; the exec, probe and spawn adapters exist and the socket unlink crash test passes; the foreground fresh-decoy table is named and passes; tests ran with tmux unresolvable and pgrep empty, so the determinism constraint is met with zero real-tmux witnesses as declared. Keep all of that. **What is rejected is five production semantic defects, and the reviewer is right about each.** Do not argue them away with more mock assertions.\\nTrunk has NOT moved. Build revision 2 on the rev1 candidate on top of the checkpoint `1ba06e9`. Do not refresh, do not rebase.\\n\\n1. **P1-A — safe-boundary success does not observe a safe boundary.** `exec.go:199` / `backend.go:observeBoundary` build the vector `wait-for -L ax-boundary-<generation>`. `-L` **acquires a channel lock**; on a fresh channel that succeeds immediately with no wrapper or provider signal whatsoever, and `observeBoundary` turns exit 0 into safe-boundary evidence. The requested provider proof kind is never passed into the observation. SPEC §4.C row 1208 and §4.E 1384 require a proof or a timeout. `TestReviewBoundaryNeedsSignal` reaches the production entry and fails on the vector; the committed `TestExecuteBoundary` scripts success for the wrong command. tmux(1) distinguishes lock acquisition from waiting for a signal. Separately, `lock-session` locks currently attached clients, whereas SPEC 1247/1383 requires rejecting **new** operator and provider input — that command alone implements no input barrier. Design and drive the actual barrier and boundary protocol, including new clients and provider inputs, and carry the requested proof kind into the observation.\\n2. **P1-B — a read-only attach returns a writable execution vector.** `ops.go:executeAttach` and `exec.go:BuildCommand(DirectiveAttachSession)` omit the input authorization when building argv, so with `input_authorized=false` Execute returns exactly the same `attach-session -t ...` vector as for true. `TestReviewReadOnlyAttach` fails through Execute. Reporting `false` in `AttachOutcome` does not enforce anything on a caller that executes the vector. Preserve the authorization at the execution boundary and prove input cannot reach the pane. tmux documents `-r` for read-only attach; another enforced mechanism is fine, an informational boolean is not.\\n3. **P1-C — probe failures manufacture absence and closure.** `backend.go:pollForAbsence`, `backend.go:terminateStale` and `status.go:probePanes` treat every nonzero exit as absence, and OSRunner converts a signal-killed child into `ExitCode=-1, nil` — so a timeout or a killed probe becomes successful closure. `TestReviewStopUnknownExit` drives `Execute(request-stop)` and gets a nil error with both `ProcessClosed` and `StoreClosed` true after a `-1` has-session result. SPEC status row 1206 says a read failure is **unknown, never absent**, and 1209-1210 require closure evidence for stop and stale termination. Distinguish positively observed absence from command, protocol, permission and cancellation failure; preserve the stderr and error classification you need to do it; and drive all three consumers. Narrowing to exit 1 alone is not sufficient unless the command failures that also exit 1 are distinguished too.\\n4. **P1-D — custody follows an intermediate symlink.** `bind.go:checkCustodyAncestors` calls `EvalSymlinks` **before** its walk, so a symlinked intermediate component disappears from the path being checked; `TestReviewCustodyIntermediateSymlink` builds an alias above the valid root and `CheckSocketCustody` returns nil. That contradicts SPEC 809-812 component-by-component no-follow custody and the function's own no-symlink claim, and it means the B19/B20 closure this leaf claimed is incomplete. Use the existing descriptor-safe traversal owner — this repository already has the openat-relative walk, and lexical containment plus a resolved path is not containment. Do not paper over arbitrary symlinks by treating them as the macOS `/tmp` convention. Re-prove the before-connect and before-bind entries and identity stability afterwards.\\n5. **P1-E — restore does not implement its required result contract.** `ops.go:executeRestore` returns only `PriorBindingID` and `RestoredParked`, leaving `OpOutcome.Binding` nil, while SPEC row 1176 requires `binding:TerminalInstanceBinding`. `TestReviewRestoreRequiredBinding` fails after a successful production Execute. \\\"No minter exists\\\" is not an external constraint that permits a different wire contract — compose the binding owner and return the required binding. And supply the after-restore ordered composition this leaf was assigned: the candidate only launches `ax pane`, unconditionally reports parked, and neither its restore witness nor its evidence drives the §4.2 1433-1441 sequence — local lease read, then a bounded refresh, then local resume / remote offer / park. Existing `axpane` decision tests do not show this new entry is wired to those effects. Preserve the ownership split, but supply the actual composition, the lapsed-local-grant remote-offer witness, and the **measured** refresh bound (a number, not the phrase \\\"without blocking forever\\\").\\n6. **P2-A — replayed outcomes change their timestamps.** `lifecycle.go:executeEngineOp` regenerates `InputClosedAt` and `BoundaryObservedAt` from `Now` when a stored mutation result is replayed; `TestReviewQuiesceReplayTimestamp` advances the clock a second between identical requests and sees different closure timestamps with no second effect. SPEC 1207 binds evidence to closure time and generation and 1208 requires the same proof on retry. Persist and replay the complete operation-specific outcome including its original timestamps, and attack the crash and retry paths rather than only exec-count equality.\\n7. **P2-B — the census misclassifies reachable cells, and the comparison is the forbidden one.** Your census defines `U2a` as \\\"entry never calls this code\\\" and then uses it for rootwrite and ancestorwrite at status and restore — but `Execute` call