\\n**Three of the five revision-1 P1s are confirmed fixed and stay fixed:** read-only attach now emits `-r` and the writable-vector defect is gone; `OSRunner` carries stderr and signal death as an error so unmarked, negative and permission probe failures no longer prove closure in the tested consumers; custody uses no-follow opens with a lexical ancestor walk and the intermediate-symlink refusal, identity seam and root/status, root/restore regressions all pass. B18, B16, the foreground fresh-decoy tests, the exec/probe/spawn adapters and the socket-unlink crash test also hold. Keep every one of them.\\n\\n**START WITH THE REFRESH — THE BRIEF'S OLD PREMISE IS NOW FALSE AND THAT IS MY DOING.** Every earlier brief on this leaf said \\\"trunk has NOT moved\\\". It has: I landed STORY-260917-kf9g1h while you were working, so `origin/main` is now **`40bb8c9f89a5430c556e8746e1dca9769f4b3c47`** and your HEAD is two commits behind it. Your candidate is still byte-identical to its checkpoint outside this leaf, but it differs from current main in sibling-owned `fencing`, `sessrepo`, `terminstance`, `axpane` and `traceability` files, and main-only tests are absent from your tree. So: run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-1c28dz` FIRST, through the managed path — do not copy old sibling files over main and do not rebase the branch by hand. Then audit **every** path in `git diff --name-only 1ba06e9 40bb8c9` that also appears in your `git status`, and restore trunk content by hand for anything this leaf does not own: a refresh preserves your working candidate byte-for-byte, which is right for your files and wrong for files trunk changed underneath you, and those read to a reviewer as deliberate reverts. `task-board.config.json` must end byte-identical to the new base. After the refresh, re-run your package tests and the whole importer closure against the NEW dependencies before you touch any finding — a fix validated against two-commit-old siblings is not validated.\\n\\n1. **P1-A — a read-only observation reopens quiesced input.** `ops.go:130` records `StateActive` unconditionally after attach, and `checkAttachQuiesced` reads that same memory. So: quiesce succeeds, an admitted read-only attach flips the memory to active, and a subsequent input-authorized attach then succeeds with a writable `attach-session -t ...` argv. `TestReviewReadonlyMustNotReopenQuiescedInput` FAILS through three `Lifecycle.Execute` calls on your unmodified candidate — a sequential bypass, not a speculative race. §4.E 1383 and the §4.D input-quiescence semantics require that observation must not reopen input. Your new committed test checks the writable refusal BEFORE the read-only observation and stops short of the bypass. Preserve the barrier across observation, retry and recovery, and commit the full three-call sequence as a regression.\\n2. **P1-B — exact-instance status manufactures matching identity from the query.** `status.go:60-78` / `backend.ObserveStatus` sets `IdentityMatch` true and fills SessionID, ImplVersion, ProtoVersion and Generation **from the request**; `list-panes` supplies only command-name rows, so nothing independently observed establishes that the requested session and version own the instance. `TestReviewStatusMustObserveIdentityNotEchoQuery` records a valid binding, then queries the same present instance under a different session ID and under implementation version `9.9.9` — both return `identity_match:true`, parked, attachable. (The unrelated protocol-major case correctly refuses; keep it as the control, it is not a bug.) §4.C 1178-1187 requires all identity members to match and prescribes the absent/non-match result. Compose the durable binding and observed-identity owner instead of echoing the request into the engine's evidence.\\n3. **P1-C — reboot restore cannot produce a binding for the new server generation.** `ops.go:409-449` commits wrapper restoration first, then reads the CREATE-time binding and requires its generation to equal the new mutation context. `TestReviewRestoreAcrossServerGeneration` drives a successful production create, reopens the reboot-scoped receipt store using your own reboot convention, changes `CurrentGeneration` and the request to `generation-after-reboot`, and drives `Execute(restore)`: the runner receives `new-session` and then restore fails with `terminal_backend_integrity_failure at restore binding image`. §4.C 1211 requires prior binding, Checkpoint **and new binding** evidence; §4.E 1387 and §4.2 require restoration after reboot. Re-reading the old binding only solves the nil result inside the old generation. Compose the binding owner to persist and return the correct successor, validate before irreversible effects where you can, and prove retry and crash recovery across a real generation change.\\n4. **P1-D — the after-restore composition is still a report with no production consumer.** `ops.go:466-513` computes a second branch decision inside `tmuxserver`, returns `resumed_locally` / `remote_offered`, and nothing outside this package consumes either; the existing wrapper decision owner is not composed. Your own wire result is always parked, no provider is launched, and the tests assert strings rather than a resume or an offer effect. That does not close rev1 P1-E or the ordered-composition assignment for §4.2 1433-1441. And `TestExecuteRestoreRemoteOfferOnLapsedGrant` **does not lapse its grant** — it uses the standard future expiry; with an actually expired grant the reviewer's probe never reaches refresh or offer (`refreshed=false`, branch nil, unauthorized expiry). **That backend refusal is correct under the separate restore-authorization contract and MUST NOT be removed to make the probe pass** — it is the proof that the claimed lapsed-grant composition is absent at the entry you chose. Put the ordered decision at the correct wrapper/composition entry and drive the effects, including the lapsed-local-grant remote offer, without weakening backend authorization. Also: the callback receives a timeout context, but your \\\"blocking-refresh\\\" test neither measures elapsed duration nor drives a production mesh adapter — report the actual measured bound and the adapter assumptions honestly.\\n5. **P2-A — corrupt outcome reads become fresh successful evidence.** `lifecycle.go:477-500` treats ANY `LookupOutcome` error like absence and ignores `RecordOutcome` errors. `TestReviewCorruptOutcomeMustNotBecomeFreshEvidence` quiesces successfully, corrupts only the persisted report, advances the clock a second and retries the identical request: Execute returns success and moves `input_closed_at` from `12:00:00.000Z` to `12:00:01.000Z` **without closing input again**. This is not the B34 crash window you declared — an existing corrupt or unreadable report is silently replaced. §4.C 1207-1208 requires closure-time and generation evidence with stable retry proof. Keep failed reads distinct from missing reports, persist authoritative operation evidence with recovery semantics, and do not repair corruption by inventing a new event time.\\n6. **P2-B — five admitting narrowings still survive, and the comparison is still keyed on test verdicts.** Each leaves its gate present and admits exactly one class at one entry, and each survives the entire committed `tmuxserver` suite twice. Ship the missing refusal witnesses and their rows, reclassify the cells the census still calls unreachable, and give the side-by-side row counts the symmetry paragraph owes. Then replace the comparison: keying on test pass/fail verdicts is not an outcome grid, and the importer closure is 34 packages, not one. The sibling Story delivered exactly this and it was accepted — a runtime grid of actual return outcomes over the mechanically derived closure, with the instrumentation patches attached.\\n7. **Prose.** README's unconditional quiesce and replay claims must be corrected to match items 1 and 5. B33 already admits the wrapper never emits the boundary signal today and the timeout is fail-closed — keep that honest and do not describe it as a complete effect-level boundary protocol. B27 (production→Acquire composition) stays a bound, not a silent proof.\\n\\nPROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property: every rule driven to its effect with tmux unresolvable. After the refresh and the fixes, run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` at the NEW base. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table, the refresh audit (every path in `git diff --name-only 1ba06e9 40bb8c9` that intersected your candidate and what you did with it), the reclassified census with its ratio and symmetry counts, and the runtime importer-closure grid; update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive, attach the results standalone, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 3 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in; if the four P1s cannot all be closed, hand off a reviewable slice and enumerate precisely what remains. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\\nREJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\\n(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\\n(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\\n(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\\n(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\\n(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\\n(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \\\"Composes X\\\", \\\"never admits what decoding refuses\\\", \\\"nothing re-implemented\\\", \\\"no cached sentinel can authorize\\\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\\nFinally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 35661,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 8916,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","final_result":true}}}
